pivot_wider — demo

arm_timepoint &lt;- table_02 |&gt;
  group_by(arm, time_point) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_ph = mean(ph))

arm_timepoint |&gt;
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = time_point,
    values_from = mean_ph
  )

# A tibble: 2 × 4
# Groups:   arm [2]
  arm       baseline week_1 week_7
  &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;
1 placebo       5.09   4.82   4.73
2 treatment     4.54   3.51   3.90


6 → 2 rows. Row = arm. Three new columns, one per visit. Useful for table-style display.

The pipe in action

table_02 |&gt;
  head() |&gt;
  nrow()

[1] 6

Same question, visual form

table_02 |&gt;
  filter(time_point == "week_7") |&gt;
  ggboxplot(x = "arm", y = "ph", fill = "arm", palette = "Set1") +
  stat_compare_means(method = "wilcox.test", label.x = 1.3)


ggboxplot() is ggpubr’s themed boxplot wrapper. stat_compare_means() is a ggplot layer that adds the p-value. The table-form and figure-form of this test should give the same p-value, and they do.

The code
panel &lt;- cyt |&gt; filter(limits == "within limits")

# one paired Wilcoxon per cytokine, then Benjamini-Hochberg
panel |&gt;
  pivot_wider(id_cols = c(pid, cytokine),
              names_from = time_point, values_from = conc,
              values_fn = mean) |&gt;
  drop_na() |&gt;
  group_by(cytokine) |&gt;
  summarise(p = wilcox.test(baseline, after_antibiotic,
                            paired = TRUE)$p.value, n = n()) |&gt;
  mutate(p_BH = p.adjust(p, "BH")) |&gt;
  arrange(p_BH)

Testing a label with PERMANOVA

as_counts(table = "microbiome") |&gt;
  tax_transform("identity", rank = "species") |&gt;
  dist_calc("bray") |&gt;
  dist_permanova(variables = c("community", "outcome", "time_point", "rnaseq_batch"),
                 n_perms = 999, by = "margin")

PERMANOVA asks: if I shuffled this label across samples, how often would the groups end up this far apart by chance?

R2 is the share of the total variation that the label explains.

Now the gene table
The species code from earlier:

as_counts(table = "microbiome") |&gt;
  tax_transform("identity", rank = "species") |&gt;
  dist_calc("bray") |&gt;
  ord_calc("PCoA") |&gt;
  ord_plot(colour = "community")

The gene version filters out rare genes and swaps the transform, rank and distance. The rest is the same:

as_counts(table = "rnaseq", min_count = 10, min_samples = 100) |&gt;
  tax_transform("clr", rank = "symbol") |&gt;
  dist_calc("euclidean") |&gt;
  ord_calc("PCoA") |&gt;
  ord_plot(colour = "community")
